{"task_id": "HumanEval/24", "solution": "```python\ndef largest_divisor(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the largest proper divisor of a positive integer n.\n    This is the largest integer d such that d < n and n % d == 0.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(n, int) or n <= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input 'n' must be a positive integer.\")\n\n    if n == 1:\n        return 1\n\n    # Iterate from n // 2 down to 1 to find the largest divisor.\n    for i in range(n // 2, 0, -1):\n        if n % i == 0:\n            return i\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/24", "solution": "```python\ndef largest_divisor(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Finds the largest proper divisor of a positive integer n.\n    Raises ValueError if n is not an integer or is not greater than 1.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(n, int):\n        raise ValueError(\"Input 'n' must be an integer.\")\n    if n <= 1:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input 'n' must be greater than 1.\")\n\n    for i in range(n // 2, 0, -1):\n        if n % i == 0:\n            return i\n    # This point is logically unreachable for valid 'n' > 1, as 1 is always found by the loop.\n    # It remains here for robustness as per the design's example.\n    return 1\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/27", "solution": "```python\ndef flip_case(string: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"For a given string, flip lowercase characters to uppercase and uppercase to lowercase.\n    Non-alphabetic characters remain unchanged.\n    \"\"\"\n    flipped_chars = []\n    for char in string:\n        if char.islower():\n            flipped_chars.append(char.upper())\n        elif char.isupper():\n            flipped_chars.append(char.lower())\n        else:\n            flipped_chars.append(char)\n    return \"\".join(flipped_chars)\n```"}
