{"task_id": "HumanEval/33", "solution": "```python\ndef sort_third(l: list) -> list:\n    \"\"\"This function takes a list l and returns a list l' such that\n    l' is identical to l in the indicies that are not divisible by three, while its values at the indicies that are divisible by three are equal\n    to the values of the corresponding indicies of l, but sorted.\n    >>> sort_third([1, 2, 3])\n    [1, 2, 3]\n    >>> sort_third([5, 6, 3, 4, 8, 9, 2])\n    [2, 6, 3, 4, 8, 9, 5]\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(l, list):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input must be a list.\")\n\n    if not l:\n        return []\n\n    # Identify elements and their original indices where index is divisible by three.\n    third_elements_with_indices = []\n    for i in range(len(l)):\n        if i % 3 == 0:\n            third_elements_with_indices.append((l[i], i))\n\n    # If no such elements, return a shallow copy of the original list.\n    if not third_elements_with_indices:\n        return list(l)\n\n    # Extract values and sort them in ascending order.\n    third_elements_values = [item[0] for item in third_elements_with_indices]\n    try:\n        third_elements_values.sort()\n    except TypeError as e:\n        raise TypeError(f\"Elements at divisible-by-three indices are not comparable: {e}\")\n\n    # Create the new list as a copy of the original.\n    result_list = list(l)\n\n    # Place the sorted elements back into their original divisible-by-three positions.\n    third_indices = [item[1] for item in third_elements_with_indices]\n    for i, sorted_val in zip(third_indices, third_elements_values):\n        result_list[i] = sorted_val\n\n    return result_list\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/35", "solution": "```python\nclass EmptyListError(Exception):\n    \"\"\"Custom exception raised when an operation is performed on an empty list.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, message: str = \"Input list cannot be empty.\"):\n        self.message = message\n        super().__init__(self.message)\n\ndef max_element(l: list):\n    \"\"\"Return maximum element in the list.\n    >>> max_element([1, 2, 3])\n    3\n    >>> max_element([5, 3, -5, 2, -3, 3, 9, 0, 123, 1, -10])\n    123\n    \"\"\"\n    if not l:\n        raise EmptyListError(\"Input list cannot be empty.\")\n\n    max_value = l[0]\n    for element in l[1:]:\n        if element > max_value:\n            max_value = element\n    return max_value\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/35", "solution": "```python\ndef max_element(l: list):\n    \"\"\"Return the maximum element in the list.\n    Raises ValueError if the input list is empty.\n    >>> max_element([1, 2, 3])\n    3\n    >>> max_element([5, 3, -5, 2, -3, 3, 9, 0, 123, 1, -10])\n    123\n    \"\"\"\n    if not l:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input list cannot be empty.\")\n    \n    # Initialize the maximum value with the first element of the list.\n    max_val = l[0]\n    \n    # Iterate through the remaining elements to find a larger value.\n    for element in l[1:]:\n        if element > max_val:\n            max_val = element\n            \n    return max_val\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/33", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List, Any\n\nclass ListValidator:\n    \"\"\"Utility class for validating list inputs and element sortability.\"\"\"\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def validate_list_input(l: Any) -> None:\n        \"\"\"\n        Validates if the input 'l' is a Python list.\n        Raises TypeError if 'l' is not a list.\n        \"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(l, list):\n            raise TypeError(\"Input must be a list.\")\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def validate_sortable_elements(elements: List[Any]) -> None:\n        \"\"\"\n        Validates if all elements in the given list are sortable.\n        Relies on Python's built-in sorted() to raise TypeError if elements are not comparable.\n        \"\"\"\n        try:\n            _ = sorted(elements)\n        except TypeError as e:\n            raise TypeError(f\"Elements in the list are not sortable: {e}\") from e\n\nclass ListSorter:\n    \"\"\"\n    Provides functionality to sort elements at indices divisible by three in a list.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def sort_third(l: List[Any]) -> List[Any]:\n        \"\"\"\n        This function takes a list l and returns a list l' such that\n        l' is identical to l in the indicies that are not divisible by three,\n        while its values at the indicies that are divisible by three are equal\n        to the values of the corresponding indicies of l, but sorted.\n        \"\"\"\n        ListValidator.validate_list_input(l)\n\n        if not l:\n            return []\n\n        # Identify indices divisible by three (0, 3, 6, ...)\n        third_indices = [i for i in range(len(l)) if i % 3 == 0]\n        \n        # Extract and sort elements at these identified indices\n        elements_to_sort = [l[i] for i in third_indices]\n        ListValidator.validate_sortable_elements(elements_to_sort)\n        sorted_elements = sorted(elements_to_sort)\n\n        # Create a shallow copy and replace elements at identified indices with sorted values\n        result_list = list(l)\n        for i, original_index in enumerate(third_indices):\n            result_list[original_index] = sorted_elements[i]\n            \n        return result_list\n\n# Expose the sort_third function as required by the original problem statement,\n# referring to the ListSorter's static method.\ndef sort_third(l: List[Any]) -> List[Any]:\n    \"\"\"\n    This function takes a list l and returns a list l' such that\n    l' is identical to l in the indicies that are not divisible by three,\n    while its values at the indicies that are divisible by three are equal\n    to the values of the corresponding indicies of l, but sorted.\n\n    >>> sort_third([1, 2, 3])\n    [1, 2, 3]\n    >>> sort_third([5, 6, 3, 4, 8, 9, 2])\n    [2, 6, 3, 4, 8, 9, 5]\n    \"\"\"\n    return ListSorter.sort_third(l)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/36", "solution": "def fizz_buzz(n: int):\n    \"\"\"Return the number of times the digit 7 appears in integers less than n which are divisible by 11 or 13.\"\"\"\n\n    # Helper function to count occurrences of a specific digit within a number.\n    def _count_digit_occurrences(number: int, digit: int) -> int:\n        return str(number).count(str(digit))\n\n    # Validate input 'n' as per FR1 and test cases.\n    if not isinstance(n, int):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input 'n' must be an integer.\")\n    if n <= 0:  # Handles negative n and n=0 as per design's test cases.\n        raise ValueError(\"Input 'n' must be a positive integer.\")\n\n    TARGET_DIGIT = 7\n    total_count = 0\n\n    # Iterate through all positive integers i such that 1 <= i < n.\n    for i in range(1, n):\n        # Determine if i is divisible by 11 or by 13.\n        if i % 11 == 0 or i % 13 == 0:\n            # Count occurrences of '7' and add to total.\n            total_count += _count_digit_occurrences(i, TARGET_DIGIT)\n\n    # Return the total accumulated count of the digit '7'.\n    return total_count"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/36", "solution": "```python\ndef fizz_buzz(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Return the number of times the digit 7 appears in integers less than n which are divisible by 11 or 13.\n    It validates input n and processes numbers from 1 to n-1.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(n, int) or n < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input 'n' must be a non-negative integer.\")\n\n    total_sevens = 0\n    for i in range(1, n):\n        # Check if i is divisible by 11 or 13.\n        if (i % 11 == 0) or (i % 13 == 0):\n            # Count occurrences of the digit '7' in the string representation of i.\n            total_sevens += str(i).count('7')\n    return total_sevens\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/37", "solution": "```python\ndef sort_even(l: list) -> list:\n    \"\"\"This function takes a list l and returns a list l' such that\n    l' is identical to l in the odd indicies, while its values at the even indicies are equal\n    to the values of the even indicies of l, but sorted.\n    >>> sort_even([1, 2, 3])\n    [1, 2, 3]\n    >>> sort_even([5, 6, 3, 4])\n    [3, 6, 5, 4]\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(l, list):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input must be a list.\")\n\n    if not l:\n        return []\n\n    even_elements = []\n    for i in range(0, len(l), 2):\n        even_elements.append(l[i])\n\n    even_elements.sort()\n\n    result = list(l) # Create a mutable copy to avoid modifying the original list.\n    even_idx_counter = 0\n    for i in range(0, len(result), 2):\n        result[i] = even_elements[even_idx_counter]\n        even_idx_counter += 1\n\n    return result\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/37", "solution": "```python\ndef sort_even(l: list) -> list:\n    \"\"\"\n    This function takes a list l and returns a list l' such that\n    l' is identical to l in the odd indicies, while its values at the even indicies are equal\n    to the values of the even indicies of l, but sorted.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not l:\n        return []\n\n    even_elements = [l[i] for i in range(0, len(l), 2)]\n    sorted_even_elements = sorted(even_elements)\n\n    result = l[:]  # Create a shallow copy to preserve odd elements.\n    even_idx_counter = 0\n    for i in range(0, len(l), 2):\n        result[i] = sorted_even_elements[even_idx_counter]\n        even_idx_counter += 1\n\n    return result\n```"}
