{"task_id": "HumanEval/32", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\n# Internal constants for the find_zero function, derived from the design specification.\n# These define the default search range, tolerance, and maximum iterations for the bisection method.\n_BISECTION_DEFAULT_TOL: float = 1e-6\n_BISECTION_MAX_ITER: int = 100\n_BISECTION_SEARCH_RANGE_START: float = -100.0\n_BISECTION_SEARCH_RANGE_END: float = 100.0\n\n\ndef poly(xs: list, x: float):\n    \"\"\"\n    Evaluates polynomial with coefficients xs at point x.\n    return xs[0] + xs[1] * x + xs[1] * x^2 + .... xs[n] * x^n\n    \"\"\"\n    return sum([coeff * math.pow(x, i) for i, coeff in enumerate(xs)])\n\n\ndef find_zero(xs: list):\n    \"\"\" xs are coefficients of a polynomial.\n    find_zero find x such that poly(x) = 0.\n    find_zero returns only only zero point, even if there are many.\n    Moreover, find_zero only takes list xs having even number of coefficients\n    and largest non zero coefficient as it guarantees\n    a solution.\n    >>> round(find_zero([1, 2]), 2) # f(x) = 1 + 2x\n    -0.5\n    >>> round(find_zero([-6, 11, -6, 1]), 2) # (x - 1) * (x - 2) * (x - 3) = -6 + 11x - 6x^2 + x^3\n    1.0\n    \"\"\"\n    if not xs:\n        raise ValueError(\"Coefficients list cannot be empty.\")\n    if len(xs) % 2 != 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Coefficients list must have an even number of elements for root finding.\")\n\n    a = _BISECTION_SEARCH_RANGE_START\n    b = _BISECTION_SEARCH_RANGE_END\n    tol = _BISECTION_DEFAULT_TOL\n    max_iter = _BISECTION_MAX_ITER\n\n    val_a = poly(xs, a)\n    val_b = poly(xs, b)\n\n    if val_a * val_b >= 0:\n        # Check if either endpoint is already a root within tolerance.\n        if abs(val_a) < tol:\n            return a\n        if abs(val_b) < tol:\n            return b\n        # If no root at endpoints and no sign change, bisection is not applicable.\n        # Given the problem's guarantee for even number of coefficients (odd degree),\n        # a sufficiently large range should always yield a sign change.\n        # If this exception is raised, it might indicate an extremely large root\n        # outside the default search range or numerical instability.\n        raise RuntimeError(\n            \"Function has same sign at interval endpoints, bisection method not applicable within default range.\"\n        )\n\n    low = a\n    high = b\n    val_low = val_a\n    val_high = val_b\n\n    for _ in range(max_iter):\n        mid = (low + high) / 2\n        val_mid = poly(xs, mid)\n\n        if abs(val_mid) < tol:\n            return mid\n\n        if val_low * val_mid < 0:\n            high = mid\n            val_high = val_mid\n        else:\n            low = mid\n            val_low = val_mid\n\n    # If the loop completes without finding a root within tolerance,\n    # return the midpoint of the last interval as the best approximation.\n    # This aligns with returning \"only one zero point\" even if full convergence\n    # within max_iter was not met, providing an approximate solution.\n    return (low + high) / 2\n\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/57", "solution": "```python\nclass MonotonicCheckerException(TypeError):\n    \"\"\"Custom exception for MonotonicChecker errors.\"\"\"\n    pass\n\nclass MonotonicChecker:\n    \"\"\"\n    Provides static methods to determine if a given list of numbers is monotonically increasing or decreasing.\n    It handles input validation and delegates specific checks.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def _validate_input(l: list) -> None:\n        \"\"\"\n        Internal helper method to validate that the input `l` is a list and contains only numeric elements.\n        Raises MonotonicCheckerException if validation fails.\n        \"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(l, list):\n            raise MonotonicCheckerException(\"Input must be a list.\")\n        for item in l:\n            if not isinstance(item, (int, float)):\n                raise MonotonicCheckerException(\"List elements must be numbers (int or float).\")\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def _is_increasing(l: list) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Internal helper method to check if the list is non-decreasing.\"\"\"\n        return all(l[i] <= l[i+1] for i in range(len(l) - 1))\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def _is_decreasing(l: list) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Internal helper method to check if the list is non-increasing.\"\"\"\n        return all(l[i] >= l[i+1] for i in range(len(l) - 1))\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def is_monotonic(l: list) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"\n        The public interface to check for monotonicity.\n        It first validates input, then checks if the list is either increasing or decreasing.\n        \"\"\"\n        MonotonicChecker._validate_input(l)\n        if len(l) <= 1:\n            return True\n        return MonotonicChecker._is_increasing(l) or MonotonicChecker._is_decreasing(l)\n\ndef monotonic(l: list):\n    \"\"\"Return True is list elements are monotonically increasing or decreasing.\n    >>> monotonic([1, 2, 4, 20])\n    True\n    >>> monotonic([1, 20, 4, 10])\n    False\n    >>> monotonic([4, 1, 0, -10])\n    True\n    \"\"\"\n    return MonotonicChecker.is_monotonic(l)\n```"}
