{"task_id": "HumanEval/96", "solution": "```python\ndef count_up_to(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates and returns a list of prime numbers strictly less than n.\n    Handles input validation and edge cases for n.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Validate input n: must be a non-negative integer.\n    if not isinstance(n, int):\n        raise ValueError(\"Input 'n' must be an integer.\")\n    if n < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input 'n' must be a non-negative integer.\")\n\n    # Return empty list for n <= 2 as there are no primes strictly less than these.\n    if n <= 2:\n        return []\n\n    # Use Sieve of Eratosthenes for efficient prime generation up to n.\n    # Initialize a boolean array to mark primality of numbers up to n-1.\n    is_prime = [True] * n\n    is_prime[0] = is_prime[1] = False # 0 and 1 are not prime.\n\n    # Iterate from 2 up to sqrt(n) to mark multiples.\n    # If a number is prime, mark all its multiples as non-prime.\n    for p in range(2, int(n**0.5) + 1):\n        if is_prime[p]:\n            for multiple in range(p * p, n, p):\n                is_prime[multiple] = False\n\n    # Collect all numbers marked as prime strictly less than n.\n    # These are naturally in ascending order due to the iteration.\n    primes = []\n    for num in range(2, n):\n        if is_prime[num]:\n            primes.append(num)\n\n    return primes\n```"}
