{"task_id": "HumanEval/130", "solution": "```python\ndef tri(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the first n + 1 Tribonacci numbers.\n    Handles base cases and uses a two-pass approach for recurrence relations.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input 'n' must be a non-negative integer.\")\n\n    if n == 0:\n        return [1.0]\n\n    # Initialize sequence array, size n + 2 to accommodate tri(n+1) for odd n calculation.\n    sequence = [0.0] * (n + 2)\n    sequence[0] = 1.0\n    sequence[1] = 3.0\n\n    # First pass: Calculate all even terms up to n + 1.\n    for k in range(2, n + 2):\n        if k % 2 == 0:\n            sequence[k] = 1.0 + k / 2.0\n\n    # Second pass: Calculate odd terms from 3 up to n.\n    for k in range(3, n + 1):\n        if k % 2 != 0:\n            # tri(k) = tri(k - 1) + tri(k - 2) + tri(k + 1)\n            sequence[k] = sequence[k - 1] + sequence[k - 2] + sequence[k + 1]\n\n    # Return the first n + 1 numbers [tri(0), ..., tri(n)].\n    return sequence[:n + 1]\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/129", "solution": "```python\ndef minPath(grid, k):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a grid with N rows and N columns (N >= 2) and a positive integer k, \n    each cell of the grid contains a value. Every integer in the range [1, N * N]\n    inclusive appears exactly once on the cells of the grid.\n\n    You have to find the minimum path of length k in the grid. You can start\n    from any cell, and in each step you can move to any of the neighbor cells,\n    in other words, you can go to cells which share an edge with you current\n    cell.\n    Please note that a path of length k means visiting exactly k cells (not\n    necessarily distinct).\n    You CANNOT go off the grid.\n    A path A (of length k) is considered less than a path B (of length k) if\n    after making the ordered lists of the values on the cells that A and B go\n    through (let's call them lst_A and lst_B), lst_A is lexicographically less\n    than lst_B, in other words, there exist an integer index i (1 <= i <= k)\n    such that lst_A[i] < lst_B[i] and for any j (1 <= j < i) we have\n    lst_A[j] = lst_B[j].\n    It is guaranteed that the answer is unique.\n    Return an ordered list of the values on the cells that the minimum path go through.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    # --- Utility Constants and Functions ---\n    DIRECTIONS = [(0, 1), (0, -1), (1, 0), (-1, 0)]  # Right, Left, Down, Up\n\n    def is_valid_coordinate(r: int, c: int, N: int) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Checks if coordinates are within grid bounds.\"\"\"\n        return 0 <= r < N and 0 <= c < N\n\n    # --- Data Models ---\n    class Cell:\n        \"\"\"Represents a single cell in the grid.\"\"\"\n        def __init__(self, row: int, col: int, value: int):\n            self.row = row\n            self.col = col\n            self.value = value\n\n        def __eq__(self, other: object) -> bool:\n            if not isinstance(other, Cell):\n                return NotImplemented\n            return self.row == other.row and self.col == other.col and self.value == other.value\n\n        def __hash__(self) -> int:\n            return hash((self.row, self.col, self.value))\n\n    class Grid:\n        \"\"\"Represents the N x N grid and provides cell value access.\"\"\"\n        def __init__(self, grid_values: list[list[int]]):\n            self._N = len(grid_values)\n            self._grid_values = grid_values\n\n        def get_value(self, r: int, c: int) -> int:\n            return self._grid_values[r][c]\n\n        def get_N(self) -> int:\n            return self._N\n\n    class Path:\n        \"\"\"Represents a sequence of visited cells and supports lexicographical comparison.\"\"\"\n        def __init__(self, initial_cell: Cell):\n            self._cells: list[Cell] = [initial_cell]\n            self._values: list[int] = [initial_cell.value]\n\n        def add_cell(self, cell: Cell):\n            self._cells.append(cell)\n            self._values.append(cell.value)\n\n        def remove_last_cell(self):\n            if self._cells:\n                self._cells.pop()\n                self._values.pop()\n\n        def get_length(self) -> int:\n            return len(self._cells)\n\n        def get_values(self) -> list[int]:\n            return self._values\n\n        def get_last_cell(self) -> Cell:\n            return self._cells[-1] if self._cells else None\n\n        def is_lexicographically_smaller(self, other_path_values: list[int]) -> bool:\n            \"\"\"Compares path values lexicographically. Assumes paths are of equal length for final comparison.\"\"\"\n            return self._values < other_path_values\n\n    # --- Core Service Logic ---\n    class PathFinderService:\n        \"\"\"Encapsulates the core logic for finding the minimum path using DFS.\"\"\"\n        def __init__(self):\n            self._min_path_values: list[int] = []\n            self._k: int = 0\n            self._grid: Grid | None = None\n\n        def find_min_path(self, grid_values: list[list[int]], k: int) -> list[int]:\n            self._grid = Grid(grid_values)\n            self._k = k\n            # Initialize with values larger than any possible path to ensure the first valid path found becomes _min_path_values.\n            self._min_path_values = [float('inf')] * k \n\n            N = self._grid.get_N()\n\n            for r_start in range(N):\n                for c_start in range(N):\n                    initial_cell = Cell(r_start, c_start, self._grid.get_value(r_start, c_start))\n                    current_path = Path(initial_cell)\n                    self._dfs(current_path)\n            \n            return self._min_path_values\n\n        def _dfs(self, current_path: Path):\n            \"\"\"Recursively explores paths of length k, updating the minimum path found.\"\"\"\n            if current_path.get_length() == self._k:\n                if current_path.is_lexicographically_smaller(self._min_path_values):\n                    self._min_path_values = current_path.get_values()[:]  # Make a copy\n                return\n\n            # Pruning: If current path's prefix is already lexicographically greater than _min_path_values's prefix,\n            # this branch cannot lead to a smaller path.\n            if current_path.get_length() < self._k:\n                current_values = current_path.get_values()\n                if current_values >= self._min_path_values[:len(current_values)]:\n                    return\n\n            last_cell = current_path.get_last_cell()\n            N = self._grid.get_N()\n\n            for dr, dc in DIRECTIONS:\n                next_r, next_c = last_cell.row + dr, last_cell.col + dc\n                if is_valid_coordinate(next_r, next_c, N):\n                    next_cell_value = self._grid.get_value(next_r, next_c)\n                    next_cell = Cell(next_r, next_c, next_cell_value)\n\n                    current_path.add_cell(next_cell)\n                    self._dfs(current_path)\n                    current_path.remove_last_cell()  # Backtrack\n\n    # --- Main function logic ---\n    service = PathFinderService()\n    return service.find_min_path(grid, k)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/131", "solution": "```python\ndef digits(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the product of all odd digits in a given positive integer.\n    Returns 0 if no odd digits are found.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Validate input n to ensure it's a positive integer.\n    if not isinstance(n, int):\n        raise ValueError(\"Input must be an integer.\")\n    if n <= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input must be a positive integer.\")\n\n    product = 1\n    has_odd_digit = False\n\n    # Iterate through digits of the number to find and multiply odd ones.\n    for digit_char in str(n):\n        digit = int(digit_char)\n        if digit % 2 != 0:\n            product *= digit\n            has_odd_digit = True\n\n    return product if has_odd_digit else 0\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/132", "solution": "```python\nclass InvalidBracketStringError(Exception):\n    \"\"\"Custom exception for invalid bracket strings.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, message: str):\n        super().__init__(message)\n\nVALID_BRACKET_CHARS = \"[]\"\n\ndef is_nested(string: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if the input string contains a valid nested bracket subsequence,\n    defined as a subsequence of the form '[[]]'.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Validate that the input string contains only allowed bracket characters.\n    for char in string:\n        if char not in VALID_BRACKET_CHARS:\n            raise InvalidBracketStringError(\n                f\"Input string contains invalid character: '{char}'. \"\n                f\"Only '{VALID_BRACKET_CHARS}' are allowed.\"\n            )\n\n    # Search for the subsequence '[[]]' by finding four ordered indices.\n    idx1 = -1\n    for i, char in enumerate(string):\n        if char == '[':\n            idx1 = i\n            break\n    if idx1 == -1: # No first opening bracket found.\n        return False\n\n    idx2 = -1\n    for i in range(idx1 + 1, len(string)):\n        if string[i] == '[':\n            idx2 = i\n            break\n    if idx2 == -1: # No second opening bracket found after the first.\n        return False\n\n    idx3 = -1\n    for i in range(idx2 + 1, len(string)):\n        if string[i] == ']':\n            idx3 = i\n            break\n    if idx3 == -1: # No first closing bracket found after the second opening.\n        return False\n\n    idx4 = -1\n    for i in range(idx3 + 1, len(string)):\n        if string[i] == ']':\n            idx4 = i\n            break\n    if idx4 == -1: # No second closing bracket found after the first closing.\n        return False\n\n    return True\n```"}
