{"task_id": "HumanEval/142", "solution": "```python\ndef sum_squares(lst: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    This function transforms a list of integers based on index rules and returns their sum.\n    It squares elements at indices multiple of 3, cubes elements at indices multiple of 4 (but not 3), and leaves others unchanged.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not lst:\n        return 0\n\n    # Create a mutable copy to avoid modifying the original list.\n    processed_list = list(lst) \n\n    for i, value in enumerate(processed_list):\n        if i % 3 == 0:\n            processed_list[i] = value * value\n        elif i % 4 == 0: # This condition implicitly means i % 3 != 0 due to the 'elif'.\n            processed_list[i] = value * value * value\n        # else: no change needed, as per FR4.\n\n    return sum(processed_list)\n\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/143", "solution": "```python\ndef words_in_sentence(sentence: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Filters words from a sentence, returning only those whose lengths are prime numbers.\n    The order of words is maintained.\n    \"\"\"\n    def is_prime(n: int) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Checks if an integer n is a prime number.\"\"\"\n        if n < 2:\n            return False\n        for i in range(2, int(n**0.5) + 1):\n            if n % i == 0:\n                return False\n        return True\n\n    words = sentence.split(' ')\n    prime_length_words = []\n    for word in words:\n        if is_prime(len(word)):\n            prime_length_words.append(word)\n    return ' '.join(prime_length_words)\n```"}
